"""Concrete computer situations before abstract cryptographic vocabulary."""

def concrete_basics(D):
 rows=[
 (53,'A student types a report and saves it on a laptop. What information is being protected?','The contents of the saved report','The color of the laptop case|Only the name of the university|Only the shape of the keyboard','A report is information stored in a file. Names, messages, photographs, account records, and passwords are other examples of information a computer handles.'),
 (53,'Where is the saved report kept after the laptop is turned off?','On the laptop’s storage drive','Only on the screen|Only inside the keyboard|Automatically on every website','A storage drive keeps saved files. The screen displays information; it is not the place where the saved file is kept.'),
 (53,'What does an application do when the student opens the report?','It reads the stored file and processes its contents so the student can use it','It turns the physical keyboard into the file|It sends the file to every person on the Internet|It makes the file impossible to copy','An application is a program. A document editor reads the file and works with its information using the computer’s working memory.'),
 (53,'Why might the student want to protect a report containing names and private records?','To keep people without permission from reading those contents','To make the report’s title longer|To make the laptop screen larger|To stop the student ever opening a file again','The protection goal is confidentiality: authorized people can read the information, while others should not.'),
 (1,'When software encrypts the report, what is transformed?','The computer representation of the report’s contents','The physical metal of the laptop|The student’s keyboard keys|The university’s building','Computers represent file contents as data. Encryption software mathematically transforms data; it does not alter the physical laptop.'),
 (1,'What performs the encryption operation on the computer?','Software or hardware implementing an encryption procedure','The file’s title by itself|The appearance of a padlock picture|The student merely calling the file private','An application, operating-system feature, or dedicated device can implement encryption. A security label or filename alone does not encrypt contents.'),
 (37,'An encryption procedure follows defined calculation steps. What is that procedure called?','An algorithm','The computer’s owner|A physical door lock|The title printed on a report','An algorithm is a procedure. Encryption programs implement its steps on the information being protected.'),
 (37,'Encryption software uses a cryptographic key. What is that key physically represented as in the computer?','A value represented by bits, stored or used by software or protected hardware','A physical keyboard key|A copy of the laptop’s metal house key|A special shape of mouse pointer','A cryptographic key is digital data. It is often a generated bit string. It is not the keyboard key pressed when typing.'),
 (37,'What does the key do when software encrypts a file?','It controls the particular transformation performed by the encryption procedure','It changes the size of the keyboard|It tells the printer which paper tray to use|It replaces the need for encryption software','The procedure is the algorithm; the supplied key controls its particular operation. The same procedure can work with many different keys.'),
 (37,'How can encryption software obtain a key?','It can generate key material securely or derive it using an appropriate procedure','It must always use the filename as the key|It always uses the owner’s first name|It gets security simply by choosing any short word','Secure key generation uses suitable unpredictable values. Password-based systems require an appropriate derivation process; a password and an encryption key are not automatically the same thing.'),
 (37,'Where might a secret key be handled while an encrypted file is being used?','In program memory or a protected key-handling component, depending on the system','Only in the physical keyboard|Necessarily printed on the outside of the laptop|Always publicly displayed next to the filename','Keys can exist in software memory, protected storage, or specialized hardware. The design must restrict access to secret keys.'),
 (1,'The report is readable before encryption. What is that readable version called?','Plaintext','The encryption key|The laptop’s operating system|The file’s access log','Plaintext means the readable information being encrypted. It can be a document, message, image, or other data, not just a sentence.'),
 (1,'The program encrypts the report. What is the resulting encrypted data called?','Ciphertext','The original readable plaintext|The physical storage drive|The sender’s name','Ciphertext is the encrypted representation. In real software it is bytes, not necessarily a readable string of scrambled letters.'),
 (1,'What does authorized software need to recover the readable report?','The appropriate decryption procedure and key','Only a new filename|Only a different screen color|Only the word confidential in the title','Decryption recovers plaintext from ciphertext. Which key is used depends on the encryption scheme.'),
 (38,'In shared-secret encryption, which key is used to encrypt and decrypt the file?','The same shared secret key','Any unrelated public number|Only the name of the file|A new random value unrelated to the encryption key','This is symmetric encryption. Shared-secret participants must protect that secret; public/private-key encryption is a different construction.'),
 (37,'Does changing report.docx to private-report.docx encrypt its contents?','No; renaming is different from transforming the file’s contents using encryption','Yes; any private-looking name encrypts the bytes|Yes; a longer filename is an encryption key|Yes; a new extension automatically proves secure encryption','A filename identifies the file. Encryption changes the protected data according to an encryption scheme.'),
 (53,'What is the purpose of encrypting a report saved on a laptop’s drive?','To protect the confidentiality of stored contents if an unauthorized person obtains the protected data','To guarantee that the laptop never breaks|To prove every statement in the report is true|To prevent every virus from running','This is protection of stored data, also called data at rest. Its effectiveness depends on encryption and key protection, including whether the device is locked or unlocked.'),
 (79,'A browser sends private account information to a website using HTTPS. What is being protected on that connection?','The information traveling between the browser and the website’s server','The physical shape of the Internet cables|Every saved file on every connected computer|The text before it is typed into the computer','HTTPS uses TLS to protect communication in transit. The browser and server process readable information at their endpoints.'),
 (79,'Does HTTPS mean the website cannot read the information you intentionally submit to it?','No; the intended website receives and processes the submitted information','Yes; websites can never read form submissions|Yes; encrypted communication removes all server access|Yes; the browser sends only the shape of the keyboard','Transport protection limits outsiders reading or modifying the communication. It does not prevent the intended receiving application from using the information.'),
 (32,'A malicious program records the report while it is open on an unlocked computer. Does encrypting the saved drive automatically stop that program?','No; stored-data protection does not stop every program from observing readable data in use','Yes; drive encryption stops every running program|Yes; the screen must show only ciphertext forever|Yes; encryption removes the need for permissions','A running application may already have access to readable data. Device permissions, monitoring, and other controls address this different exposure.'),
 (37,'A technician wants to check whether a downloaded installer matches the publisher’s trusted original. What information can be compared?','A computed digest of the download and a trusted reference digest','Only the colors in the download icon|Only whether both filenames contain the same number of letters|Only the name of the keyboard','This situation is about comparing file contents, not hiding them. A hash procedure computes a digest from the file’s bytes.'),
 (37,'When a program hashes the downloaded file, what does it read as input?','The file’s contents as data','Only the laptop’s brand name|The technician’s physical keyboard|Only how the icon looks on screen','Hashing operates on supplied data. Hashing a file normally means computing the result from its contents, not merely its displayed name.'),
 (37,'What does the hash program produce from the file’s contents?','A fixed-length value called a digest','An encrypted copy containing every original byte|A guarantee that the publisher is honest|A new physical storage drive','The digest is a computed summary. It is not the original file and is not a reversibly encrypted version of the file.'),
 (37,'Does calculating the file’s hash normally hide or replace the original file?','No; it computes a result while the original contents remain readable unless separately protected','Yes; calculating a hash automatically encrypts the file|Yes; the original must always be deleted|Yes; hashing changes the physical laptop','Hashing and encryption do different jobs. Computing a digest does not itself make the original confidential.'),
 (19,'The file is edited after the publisher recorded its trusted digest. What will normally happen when the edited file is hashed?','Its digest will differ from the trusted original digest','The digest must always stay the same|The edit will automatically be undone|The hash program will decrypt the original file','A secure hash is designed so changes alter the result unpredictably. A comparison can reveal different contents; collision resistance does not mean collisions are impossible.'),
 (37,'A matching digest is posted beside a download on an attacker-controlled page. Why is that alone insufficient evidence of authenticity?','The attacker may control both the file and the posted digest','A plain hash always proves who published the file|A digest is a physical identity card|A matching value always means a file is harmless','A comparison is only as trustworthy as its reference. Digital signatures and trusted distribution mechanisms address origin and trust separately.'),
 (37,'Which protection fits hiding a private report, and which fits comparing a downloaded file with a trusted original?','Encrypt the report for confidentiality; hash the download for comparison','Hash the report to make its readable original disappear; rename the download to prove it is authentic|Change both filenames and assume the contents are protected|Use only the appearance of a lock icon for both jobs','Identify the object and purpose first. Encryption protects readable contents from disclosure. A trusted hash comparison helps detect changed contents.'),
 ]
 D['orientation']=[]
 for i,(n,prompt,right,wrong,why) in enumerate(rows,1):
  D['orientation'].append(dict(id=f'425-context-{i}',source=n,section=D['questions'][n-1]['section'],q=prompt,correct=right,distractors=wrong.split('|'),answer=why,teach=why,example=why,foundation=True,orientation=True,format='mc',context='A private report is saved on a student’s laptop. Later, a separate downloaded installer is checked against a trusted original. These are different tasks: protecting contents from reading and comparing contents for changes.'))
  D['prerequisites'][f'425-context-{i}']=[]
 # Persistent setting alongside the question prevents the object and purpose
 # disappearing when a term is introduced later in the session.
 D['practicalContexts']={
 'stored':dict(object='A private report saved as a file on a laptop.',location='The laptop’s storage drive; readable contents may also be in working memory while open.',purpose='Keep people without permission from reading the saved contents.',action='Encryption software transforms the contents using an algorithm and key. Authorized decryption recovers the readable contents.'),
 'hash':dict(object='A downloaded file being compared with a trusted original.',location='The downloaded file is stored on the computer; a program reads its contents to compute a result.',purpose='Check whether the contents match the trusted original.',action='A hash function computes a digest from the file’s bytes. Compare it with a trusted reference; this does not encrypt the file.'),
 'transit':dict(object='Account information sent from a browser to a website.',location='The communication between the browser and the website’s server.',purpose='Protect the communication from outsiders reading or changing it.',action='HTTPS uses TLS to protect the connection. The intended endpoints still process the readable information.'),
 'signature':dict(object='A document whose recipient needs evidence of its origin and whether it changed.',location='The document and its accompanying digital signature.',purpose='Check signed content using a trusted link to the sender’s public key.',action='The sender signs using a private signing key. The recipient verifies with the corresponding trusted public key. Signing does not itself hide the document.'),
 'network':dict(object='Computers communicating with each other and the Internet.',location='Device connections, network equipment, and the records collected from them.',purpose='Allow necessary communication, block unwanted activity, and collect useful evidence.',action='Different controls filter traffic, inspect requests, detect activity, or restrict program execution; they perform different jobs.'),
 'attack':dict(object='An employee’s computer, account, and information it can access.',location='Messages, applications, login pages, and programs running on the device.',purpose='Understand how an attacker gains access or causes harm and which protection addresses that action.',action='Identify what actually happened before choosing a defense; a stolen password and malicious code execution are different events.'),
 'blockchain':dict(object='Records authorizing and tracking transfers of digital value.',location='A transaction ledger maintained and validated by participating computers.',purpose='Check spending authorization and avoid accepting conflicting spends of the same funds.',action='Signatures authorize transactions; hashes link or summarize records; validation and consensus determine accepted history.'),
 }
 for x in D['questions']+D['checks']+D['vocab']+D['foundations']:
  term=x.get('term','').lower();n=x.get('number',x.get('source'))
  if n in [18,60,61] or 'signature' in term:x['contextKey']='signature'
  elif any(t in term for t in ['key','encrypt','cipher','plaintext']) or term in ['data','secret','algorithm']:x['contextKey']='stored'
  elif n==37 and 'key' in x.get('q','').lower() and 'hash' not in x.get('q','').lower():x['contextKey']='stored'
  elif n in [19,37,57,74] or any(t in term for t in ['hash','digest','sha-256','collision']):x['contextKey']='hash'
  elif n in [20,21,40,41,75,76,14]:x['contextKey']='blockchain'
  elif n in [53,1,38,39,45,54,55,56,58,59,62,63,64] or any(t in term for t in ['key','encrypt','cipher','plaintext']):x['contextKey']='stored'
  elif n in [79,11,42,77]:x['contextKey']='transit'
  elif x['section'] in [2,8]:x['contextKey']='network'
  else:x['contextKey']='attack'
 D['counts']['orientation']=len(D['orientation'])
